# Why This Fear?
Tamil title: ஏன் இந்த அச்சம்?

By the end of this year a new Constitution will have taken shape, and it is now certain that elections will be held at the beginning of next year. No one can deny that the principles of democracy have not taken deep root across this vast continent. Democracy blossomed in the West; even among European countries other than England, it has not yet attained full growth. In a country long accustomed to dividing people by birth, inventing superiority and inferiority, declaring this to be God's command, dragging in Krishna's teaching as testimony, and treating a fellow human being worse than an animal, democracy cannot be made real merely by wishing for it. If a sound democratic order is to be established, everyone concerned with the people's advancement must labour strenuously to spread among them the knowledge it requires.

The first requirement is that the people understand the needs of democracy, possess education, and not be deceived by appearances. Our people have very little of this elementary preparation. The chief reason the Congress obtained the opportunity to rule was that it turned the people's religious faith, and their devotional attraction to outward forms and incarnations, to its own advantage.

During the freedom struggle, people loved and supported the freedom fighters after seeing their garb and hearing their slogans. They did not entrust their own sovereign authority to the Congress because they knew its leaders' governing ability or intellectual capacity, or because experience had proved them truthful men who would fulfil their promises.

Congress leaders founded a devotional monastery opposed to the white rulers. Those who had formerly gathered with cymbals in temples of Vishnu and halls of devotional song moved into this national monastery. The national force did not assemble after understanding the injuries caused by the country's being trapped in the white man's grip and courageously resolving to fight to remove them. Therefore, even when the thought arises, "Was the white man driven out only to make room for the plunderer?", it is difficult for that thought to remain firmly in the people's minds.

Recruiting followers to a new monastery is not easy. The mere noise of drums cannot attract the people. One must add more ghee, melted from a little butter, to the white pongal. Only then will devotees forget the old monastery briefly and turn towards the new one, if only for a moment.

When they lacked power, they offered countless seductive promises to recruit people: if the white man left, poverty would disappear, everyone would eat their fill, and much would be done to end hunger. Those now grown weighty in office were then the presiding deities of the national monastery. The white man departed, and they now sit in office with pomp. The pleasant life promised to the people has not been given. Unable to reveal their incapacity, they offer one excuse after another. The people are disappointed and irritated. Opposition, reproach, and questioning have arisen. With dignity in their voices, fear cast away, and without trembling before repression, the people sharply question those who came to rule. This creates fear among the rulers and among powerful men such as Kamaraj who placed them upon the throne.

He frets that the people who once showed affection now speak in condemnation; that those who once welcomed them now sing songs of abuse; and that those who once scattered flowers now hurl burning words. He pounces upon someone, crying that rebellion is being fomented. He threatens to use the same authority and send forces to suppress it. Then he laments: the election is approaching, will we win? The very thought increases his anger. He drags the Dravidar Kazhagam into a quarrel and, as though authority were his hereditary property, charges us with craving power and abandoning our objective.

We have not altered the Salem resolution. Our leader has repeatedly said that intervention in electoral politics is not our policy. We too have written many times, without leaving room for doubt even among ordinary people, that merely obtaining office is not our aim. Why has he still not acquired this understanding? He continues to speak from an old world and to be afraid.

Our objective may frighten him from time to time. Because he possesses the authority to order others along his chosen road, however, he can speak of us with contempt. He cannot comprehend the full form of our objective; that requires a little more knowledge and ability. Even Kamaraj might succeed if he attempted it, but his present status prevents him from taking that road. Forgetting that his status is the product of our agitation, he vainly believes it is a reward for his own sacrifice and skill.

As the election draws nearer, his present fear and terror will multiply. We shall not be surprised or angry if his speech then loses judgement, his arrogance increases, and he forgets ordinary human qualities. That is all we can expect from him.

Whose sacred congregation is the Congress today? How many within it serve without thought of reward? How many are prepared to leap into the fire of sacrifice? How many still believe in Gandhi's constructive programme and work for it? What is the number of virtuous workers carrying spinning wheels and spindles in service? Refraining from asking these questions is now treated as the mark of accomplished patriotism.

Those chasing power; those competing for ministerial office; those roaming in jealousy after disappointment when it is denied; those bullying officials to obtain favours; "sacrificers" who fatten themselves shamelessly upon bribes; those grinning for permits; those hanging out their tongues for licences while intimidating the rulers; those living delicately but dishonestly by promising to secure contracts; money seekers, conspirators, the immoral, and those who live by treating falsehood as truth: has not the Congress become a service ashram crowded with these and many more of their kind? None of this is an invention trumpeted by us.

"If every institution filled with bribery and corruption were dissolved, the first to be dissolved would have to be our Central Provinces government. The ministers occupying office there would have to hand the keys of the treasury to others and leave."

Thus writes *Kalachakkaram*, a Congress journal from Coimbatore.

"The saying that power corrupts politics has long been famous. We cannot deny that the moment we came into contact with power it corrupted us too. Congressmen can no longer claim to occupy the lofty pedestal of impeccable conduct. The resolution on discipline is a warning to every Congressman, including Pandit Nehru, the greatest among Congress leaders. Every word in that resolution is true."

At the Jaipur conference, Congress secretary Shankarrao Deo spoke in this forceful fashion during the debate on discipline.

Pattabhi Sitaramayya, president of the All India Congress Committee and a paragon who had once upheld the elevation of his discipline in Vellore prison, sent a circular on 17 July 1949 to wipe away the stains that had entered the Congress organisation and restore its purity:

"Complaints about bribery and illicit trade are heard everywhere. Their efforts can succeed only if they themselves, the Congressmen, are beyond anyone's suspicion. Yet the very people who say these two corrupt practices exist are involved in bribery and black-market dealings ranging from fountain pens to motor cars."

Pattabhi says that those engaged in bribery and the black market are themselves the ones who complain most about it.

[The sixth source scan repeats most of the preceding material. Its transition into the next quotation is damaged in the OCR.]

Pattabhi's report charges that Congressmen do not stop with approaching and pressuring ministers to secure jobs for relatives and friends. They also intimidate police officers and interfere in criminal cases, thereby undermining justice and integrity. It directs them not to seek recommendations from police officers, judges, administrators, or other officials in matters connected with civil or criminal court cases.

Even if people fail to obtain jobs under Congress rule, are denied other facilities, or see many hardships remain unresolved, it would at least be valuable if they could trust that equal justice was available. The patriots are intent upon destroying even that confidence. These men of sacrifice seem to have resolved that justice and honesty too may be sacrificed.

Pattabhi's report clearly reveals the degraded condition the Congress has reached and the conduct of those within it:

"The Congress, which remained united during the struggle, developed rivalry after independence. Members of both factions approach ministers and officials with improper recommendations that suit their own wishes. From an ordinary matter such as obtaining a licence to build a cinema theatre onward, occasions arise in every matter for divisions to develop among ministers and administrators. If Congressmen refrain from interfering in such matters, they will help preserve unity and purity."

For private gain, Congress "sacrificers" create divisions between ministers and the administrators under their government. Look at the quality of their minds and the colour of their deeds, and then say why the administration should not become unstable. Within the very party of those who came to rule in Gandhi's name and through speeches about freedom, delicate operators who disrupt administration have multiplied. The country is deteriorating and the people's suffering is growing. The country has become the Congressmen's private money-hunting forest. Swaraj, said to be for everyone, has turned into comfortable rule for members of their own party.

Kamaraj sees that Congress has become an organisation saturated with corruption, an institution swollen with bribery, and a marketplace crowded with black marketeers. In these circumstances the approaching election fills him with fear. He cries aloud; he is afraid.

"In the next election it will not be possible to ask the public for votes merely by invoking Congress, Gandhi, and the sacrifices of the past."

Kamaraj said this in Coimbatore. Bewitched by the Congress name, people filled the yellow ballot box. The result they saw was famine, foul-smelling ration rice, worm-eaten maize, and wheat infested with beetles. Out of love for Gandhi's name they voted; the immediate reward was to find Congressmen themselves seated as sentries over the black market. Trusting in sacrifice, the people cast their votes; now they watch with pity as the sacrificers roam about as mere seekers of money. Kamaraj therefore prepares to abandon the old method and adopt a new one to beguile the people.

Whatever method Kamaraj now adopts, experience has taught the people that libertines may wander even in saffron robes, that liquor may be hidden even in the ascetic's water-pot, and that a sharp spear may be concealed within folded hands. A change of persons or clothing can no longer deceive them. Knowing their mood well, Kamaraj's panic at the word "election" and his blaming of other parties are entirely predictable. What other important work does he have? What other task can he undertake? He does what he knows and what he can, without hesitation.

Congress leaders' own statements make it clear that the party is certain to lose the election because its misconduct has deprived it of the people's affection and support. The jealousy that has arisen among Congressmen themselves will bring Congress defeat at the next election. We are not the cause of that condition.

Our sole concern is to see whether the people obtain a comfortable life, whoever governs and however they govern. Instead of distorting and attacking the Dravidar Kazhagam without rhyme or reason, Kamaraj might at least try to remove the stain upon the Congress organisation. That could keep political death away from him for a little longer.

Congress president Pattabhi himself admits that Congressmen have not learned to administer as well as they have learned to go to prison. It is unsurprising that he and his associates fail to recognize that members of the Dravidar Kazhagam are different. For us, the administration of the country is important; going to prison is not of equal importance. Because the Dravidar Kazhagam exposes the opposition party's administrative failures, the Congress government behaves as though it could conceal its corruption by imprisoning some of our people.

Our friend Kamaraj understands this well. Wherever he goes, he reminds the people of the Dravidar Kazhagam and indirectly acknowledges the truth that its members are highly capable in administration. In a voice mixed with distress he pleads, in effect: "If they come to office, we shall be unable even to look towards politics for another generation. Save us; protect our positions from harm." To keep their secret from being exposed, he disguises this plea with the cry that the Dravidar Kazhagam conducts a "campaign of hatred" and tells the public not to trust it.

Let him speak. But Kamaraj must remember one thing: the Dravidar Kazhagam has not the slightest desire for office. We remind him once more that the day is not far off when the people will learn through experience that office is not a prize awarded for having gone to prison, but an exalted position granted for administrative ability exercised in the welfare of the nation.

*Dravida Nadu*, 30 January 1949.

---NOTES---
The canonical Tamil OCR is retained verbatim, including the duplicated sixth scan page. The translation renders that duplicated passage once and marks the duplication in brackets.

The OCR loses words at several line and quotation boundaries, especially around Pattabhi Sitaramayya's circular. The English follows the recoverable syntax and identifies the damaged transition rather than inventing missing wording.
